Z||000017||JSocha||06-20-2001||12:11 AM||||This is <B>supposedly</B> the transcript of an actual radio conversation of a US naval<BR>ship and the Canadians, off the coast of Newfoundland, On the 10th of October 1995.<P><I>Before you all bash me to the validity, it came in my email from an associate of mine from CETEC Automation, Inc. in California. With that being said, enjoy, if not for the accuracy, for the humor.</I><P><BR>CANADIANS: Please divert your course 15 degrees to the south, to avoid a collision.<P>AMERICANS: Recommend you divert your course 15 degrees to the north, to avoid a collision.<P>CANADIANS: Negative. You will have to divert your course 15 degrees to the south to avoid a collision.<P>AMERICANS: This is the captain of a United States Navy ship. I say again, divert YOUR course.<P>CANADIANS: Negative. I say again, you will have to divert  your course.<P>AMERICANS: THIS IS THE AIRCRAFT CARRIER USS LINCOLN, THE SECOND LARGEST SHIP IN THE UNITED STATES ATLANTIC FLEET. WE ARE ACCOMPANIED BY THREE DESTROYERS, THREE CRUISERS AND NUMEROUS SUPPORT VESSELS. I DEMAND THAT YOU CHANGE YOUR COURSE 15 DEGREES NORTH, I SAY AGAIN, THAT'S 15 DEGREES NORTH, OR COUNTERMEASURES WILL BE UNDERTAKEN TO ENSURE THE SAFETY OF THIS SHIP.<P>CANADIANS: We are a lighthouse. Your call.<P>BTW, FH, Brown w/Hazel Eyes!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||1
